EXT. FROZEN MOUNTAIN LAKE - DAY

A vast white bowl ringed by black pines. A bush plane lies on its belly in the middle of the ice, tail snapped, one wing crumpled. Smoke curls from the engine.

Six survivors huddle around the wreck, their breath a ragged mist.

CAPTAIN ROSALIE KERR, 50s, the pilot, in a bomber jacket gone to rags, sits against the fuselage. A bloody bandage on her forearm. Her face is grey.

DR. JIN-HO PARK, 40s, in a stained parka, kneels beside her, checking her pulse. His fingers are blue.

BEATRIZ SOLANO, 30s, in a fleece, strides the perimeter with a flare gun. She scans the sky.

MR. THADDEUS OKAFOR, 60s, a retired judge in a three-piece suit and galoshes, stands rigid with his briefcase on his chest.

LENNOX BELL, 20s, a bicycle courier, perches on the broken wing, headphones around his neck.

PILAR MENDOZA-RUIZ, 30s, a student, crouches by the engine, feeding sticks into a sputtering fire.

PARK
She's bleeding too much.

BEATRIZ
Then stop it.

PARK
I'm trying.

OKAFOR
Gentlemen, ladies. Order, please.

LENNOX
Dude, there's no order. We're on a lake.

PILAR
Fire's dying.

BEATRIZ
Feed it.

PILAR
With what?

BEATRIZ
Everything.

Okafor sets down the briefcase. Opens it. Hundreds of pages of legal briefs. He tears them out in fistfuls, tosses them into the flames. They flare gold.

OKAFOR
Thirty years of my life.

LENNOX
Right on.

A distant THRUM. Everyone freezes. The sound grows. Beatriz raises the flare gun.

BEATRIZ
Quiet.

She fires. A red star climbs into the sky, hangs, drifts. The THRUM stops.

Then it starts again, closer. Louder.

PILAR
They saw it.

PARK
Don't say that yet.

A helicopter bursts over the treeline, blades flogging the pines. Lennox leaps from the wing, waving both arms. Okafor takes off his hat. Rosalie lifts her head and smiles.

KERR
(barely)
About time.

Beatriz drops the flare gun. Pilar sobs into her scarf. Park lets out a breath he has held since the crash and slumps against the fuselage.
